  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>ISO 14001:2015 requires an internal audit programme at planned intervals; the internal audit is itself an audited clause of the standard</li><li>The core areas are environmental aspects and impacts, the legal and other requirements register, objectives and targets, and corrective actions</li><li>Every nonconformity needs a corrective action with an owner and a due date, and the next audit verifies it was closed</li><li>The certification body runs surveillance audits between recertifications; your internal audit evidence is what they sample</li><li>Software does not make you certified; meeting the standard and closing your nonconformities does. The tool captures the audit and holds the evidence</li></ul></div><h2>What an ISO 14001 internal audit is for</h2><p>An ISO 14001 certificate is not earned once. It is a claim you keep proving at surveillance audits, that your environmental management system is real and running. The internal audit is how you prove it to yourself first. Done well, the certification body's visit confirms what you already knew. Done as a box-tick, it becomes the moment the auditor finds the aspects register nobody has touched in two years.</p><p>Software does not make you certified. Meeting the standard and closing your nonconformities does. What the tool does is capture the audit and keep the evidence, so the internal audit programme the standard requires is demonstrably real at the surveillance visit.</p><h2>Aspects and impacts: the register must be live</h2><p>The heart of ISO 14001 is knowing how your activities interact with the environment: the aspects (what you do, such as storing fuel or generating waste) and the impacts (what that does). The standard requires you to identify these, assess significance, and manage them. The internal audit checks the register is current for how the site operates today, not for a process you stopped running last year.</p><h2>The legal and other requirements register</h2><p>ISO 14001 requires a register of legal and other requirements and a periodic evaluation of compliance against them. For a UK site that means the environmental permit conditions, the waste duty of care in the Environmental Protection Act 1990, oil storage rules, discharge consents, and sector obligations, all tracked and evidenced. A management system that lists the law but never evaluates compliance against it has a gap the auditor will find. The permit compliance audit often forms the biggest part of this register.</p><h2>Objectives, targets and management review</h2><p>The standard expects environmental objectives and targets that are set, resourced and progressing, with a management review that feeds the next cycle. The internal audit checks objectives are more than aspiration: that there is a plan, an owner, and progress against them, reviewed by top management.</p><h2>Findings and corrective actions</h2><p>An internal audit is only worth running if the findings drive action. Each nonconformity should be graded, given a root cause, assigned an owner and a due date, and closed with evidence. The next audit verifies closure. The failure mode is a corrective action raised, filed and forgotten, which reappears as a repeat finding at the surveillance visit and dents confidence in the whole system.</p><h2>What to look for in ISO 14001 audit software</h2><ul><li><strong>Clause-aligned structure:</strong> the audit should map to the standard's areas, aspects, legal register, objectives, corrective actions</li><li><strong>Evidence at the point of audit:</strong> each finding tied to a document reference or a timestamped photo, not written up from memory</li><li><strong>Open corrective action visibility:</strong> the manager should see overdue actions on a dashboard, not lose them between cycles</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes firms for office staff and subcontractor auditors. Quickler charges per report, unlimited users on every bundle</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, auditor, area, every finding, ready for the surveillance audit</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the audit and generates the report. It does not make you certified, maintain your registers for you, or replace the certification body. Meeting ISO 14001 and closing your nonconformities makes you certified. The competent auditor carries that judgement. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What does an ISO 14001 internal audit cover?</h3><p>It checks the environmental management system against ISO 14001:2015: the aspects and impacts register, the legal and other requirements register and compliance evaluation, objectives and targets, operational controls, and whether corrective actions from the last audit were closed. It produces findings graded as conformities, observations and nonconformities.</p><h3>Is an internal audit required by ISO 14001?</h3><p>Yes. ISO 14001:2015 requires the organisation to conduct internal audits at planned intervals to check the management system conforms to the standard and to its own requirements, and is effectively implemented. The internal audit programme is itself an audited requirement, so the certification body will sample your internal audit records.</p><h3>What happens to a nonconformity found in an audit?</h3><p>Each nonconformity should be graded, given a root cause, assigned an owner and a due date, and closed with evidence. The next audit verifies closure. A corrective action that is raised and then forgotten tends to reappear as a repeat finding at the surveillance audit, which undermines confidence in the whole system.</p><h3>Can I run an ISO 14001 audit over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The auditor receives each check in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed audit report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/environmental-inspection-software-uk.html\">Environmental inspection software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/environmental-permit-compliance-audit-uk.html\">Environmental permit compliance audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/spill-response-inspection-report-uk.html\">Spill response inspection report</a></li></ul>",
